@prefix ag:     <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix mc:     <http://example.org/agora/microcontroller#> .
@prefix wokwi:  <http://example.org/agora/wokwi#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/agora/wokwi#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:    <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/agora/wokwi> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Wokwi" ;
    rdfs:comment """How a part is DRAWN, and simulated, at wokwi.com. Enough to generate a diagram.json from the wiring the world already states, so the picture is derived rather than maintained — and so the same statements that refuse a bad pin also produce a board you can run the firmware on.

It is a rendering concern and nothing else reads it. A world validates identically whether or not a part says how it draws; a part that says nothing simply does not appear.

A board's legs usually need nothing here — they carry skos:notation, and Wokwi is naming the same physical header the silkscreen is. Usually, and not always: a DevKit prints GND on three separate legs, so the silkscreen is not a unique key and Wokwi disambiguates where the board does not. wokwi:name on a PIN is that override, and it exists because the tidy version of this claim was wrong.

Note what is otherwise NOT here: the pin names of a BOARD. An ESP32's legs already carry skos:notation — D34, 3V3, GND — which is what the silkscreen says and what Wokwi calls them, because Wokwi is naming the same physical header. One of the two had to be authoritative and the silkscreen was already it.""" .

# Deliberately no rdfs:domain on any of these. They are stated on CLASSES — a DHT11 draws this
# way, all of them — and a domain axiom is an inference rule: giving one `rdfs:domain mc:Peripheral`
# would make the class infer as a peripheral and get held to a peripheral's shapes, which demand
# things only an individual has. That has already cost this project two afternoons.

:part a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ; rdfs:label "wokwi part" ;
    rdfs:comment "The part id in Wokwi's catalogue — 'wokwi-dht22', 'board-esp32-devkit-c-v4'. Free text, matched against their library rather than derived from anything here." .

:pin a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:label "wokwi pin" ;
    rdfs:comment "One leg of that part, saying which of OUR roles it answers to. The join between a role and a drawing: this world says a leg is ag:PowerPinRole, Wokwi calls that leg VCC on a DHT and COM on nothing at all." .

:name a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ; rdfs:label "wokwi pin name" ;
    rdfs:comment "What Wokwi calls it, exactly. Case matters to their renderer." .

:attrs a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ; rdfs:label "wokwi attrs" ;
    rdfs:comment "A JSON object passed through to the part unchanged — an RGB LED's common polarity, a potentiometer's range. Opaque here on purpose: encoding Wokwi's whole attribute vocabulary would be maintaining their catalogue in our graph." .
